<p>The <strong>under desk elliptical as seen on TV</strong> is the compact seated pedal machine you may remember from commercials: someone typing at a desk or relaxing on a sofa while their feet glide in a smooth oval underneath. Cubii is the brand most people associate with those ads, and it helped make seated ellipticals a mainstream product.</p>
<p>So are they worth it? For the right goal, yes. A seated elliptical adds low-impact movement to hours you would otherwise spend sitting still, and it is one of the easiest pieces of exercise equipment to use while watching TV. It will not replace a real workout, and the ads naturally show it at its best.</p>
<p>This guide explains what these machines really do, which proven models to buy, how they differ from seated steppers and "ellipticals with a TV," and how to set one up for comfortable pedaling in front of the screen.</p>

<h2>What Is the Under Desk Elliptical As Seen on TV?</h2>
<p>An under desk elliptical is a small, low-profile trainer with two pedals and a flywheel. You sit in a normal chair and move your feet in an elliptical path, either against adjustable resistance (manual models) or with help from a motor (motorized models). It slides under a desk or sits in front of a sofa.</p>
<p>The models shown in TV commercials are manual, quiet and compact, with a small display for strides and estimated calories. Cubii's ads leaned on the idea that you can move while doing everyday things such as answering email or watching a show. That is a fair description of what the product does.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>In short:</strong> the as-seen-on-TV under desk elliptical is a seated, low-impact leg exerciser. It is about moving more during the day, not about getting fit fast.</p></div>

<h2>Is an Under Desk Elliptical As Seen on TV Worth It?</h2>
<p>TV advertising is good at making a product look effortless and transformative. Here is a more balanced view, based on specs and what owners report.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>What the ads get right</th><th>What the ads leave out</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>You can use it while you work or watch TV</td><td>Calorie burn is modest, roughly 100 to 200 per hour for most people</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is low impact and easy on joints</td><td>It does not replace cardio workouts or strength training</td></tr>
<tr><td>Good models are quiet and compact</td><td>Knees can hit low desks, and light machines can slide</td></tr>
<tr><td>It suits beginners and older adults</td><td>Wrist trackers often miss seated pedaling</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Who it is worth it for</h3>
<ul>
<li>Desk workers and remote workers who sit for long stretches.</li>
<li>People who want gentle, joint-friendly movement, including many seniors. Check with your doctor first if you have a health condition.</li>
<li>Anyone who already spends evenings in front of the TV and wants to move during that time.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Who should skip it</h3>
<ul>
<li>People who want a serious calorie burn or to train for fitness goals.</li>
<li>Anyone hoping to stand on it. These are seated machines.</li>
<li>Buyers expecting dramatic weight loss without changes to diet or other activity. Read <a href="/can-you-lose-weight-with-under-desk-elliptical/">can you lose weight with an under desk elliptical</a> for realistic expectations.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Best Under Desk Ellipticals From TV and Beyond</h2>
<p>You do not need the exact model from a commercial. These are the seated ellipticals we recommend, based on specs and owner reviews, including the Cubii that made the category famous.</p>
